The claim is **TRUE**. Here is the proof.

---

**Claim.** Let $\mu$ be a Borel probability measure with compact support on $\mathbb{R}$. Then $\mu$ is not a Dirac measure if and only if $|\hat{\mu}(\xi)|\not\equiv 1$ for $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$.

We use the convention $\hat{\mu}(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb{R}}e^{i\xi x}\,d\mu(x)$. We prove both directions.

---

### Direction 1 (Dirac $\Rightarrow$ $|\hat{\mu}|\equiv 1$), i.e. the contrapositive of "$\mu$ not Dirac $\Rightarrow$ $|\hat{\mu}|\not\equiv 1$".

If $\mu=\delta_{a}$ for some $a\in\mathbb{R}$, then
$$\hat{\mu}(\xi)=\int e^{i\xi x}\,d\delta_{a}(x)=e^{i\xi a},$$
so $|\hat{\mu}(\xi)|=|e^{i\xi a}|=1$ for every $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$. Hence $|\hat{\mu}|\equiv 1$. ∎

---

### Direction 2 ($|\hat{\mu}|\equiv 1$ $\Rightarrow$ $\mu$ is Dirac), i.e. the contrapositive of "$\mu$ not Dirac $\Rightarrow$ $|\hat{\mu}|\not\equiv 1$".

Assume $|\hat{\mu}(\xi)|=1$ for all $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$. Then
$$1=|\hat{\mu}(\xi)|^{2}=\hat{\mu}(\xi)\,\overline{\hat{\mu}(\xi)}=\iint e^{i\xi x}\,e^{-i\xi y}\,d\mu(x)\,d\mu(y)=\iint e^{i\xi(x-y)}\,d(\mu\otimes\mu)(x,y).$$

Taking real parts and using $\operatorname{Re} e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta$,
$$\iint \cos\!\big(\xi(x-y)\big)\,d(\mu\otimes\mu)(x,y)=1\qquad\text{for all }\xi\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{$\ast$}$$

Since $\cos\le 1$ everywhere and $(\mu\otimes\mu)$ is a probability measure, the integral in $(\ast)$ can equal $1$ only if the integrand equals $1$ almost everywhere. Concretely, for each fixed $\xi$,
$$E_{\xi}:=\{(x,y):\cos(\xi(x-y))\neq 1\}\quad\text{satisfies}\quad(\mu\otimes\mu)(E_{\xi})=0.$$

Now take the countable intersection over all rational $\xi$. Since $\mathbb{Q}$ is countable,
$$(\mu\otimes\mu)\!\left(\bigcup_{q\in\mathbb{Q}}E_{q}\right)=0,$$
so for $(\mu\otimes\mu)$-a.e. $(x,y)$ we have $\cos(q(x-y))=1$, i.e. $q(x-y)\in 2\pi\mathbb{Z}$, **for every** $q\in\mathbb{Q}$.

We claim this forces $x=y$. Indeed, suppose $x\neq y$. Choose a rational $q$ with $0<|q|<2\pi/|x-y|$. Then $0<|q(x-y)|<2\pi$, yet $q(x-y)\in 2\pi\mathbb{Z}$, which is impossible. Hence $x=y$.

Therefore
$$(\mu\otimes\mu)\big(\{(x,y):x=y\}\big)=1: \tag{$\dagger$}$$
the diagonal has full $(\mu\otimes\mu)$-measure.

**The diagonal having full measure implies $\mu$ is a Dirac measure.** Suppose, for contradiction, that $\mu$ is not a Dirac measure. Then $\mu$ is not concentrated at a single point, so there exist two disjoint Borel sets $A,B\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ with $\mu(A)>0$ and $\mu(B)>0$. (For instance: if $\mu$ has an atom at some $a$ with $0<\mu(\{a\})<1$, take $A=\{a\}$, $B=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{a\}$; if $\mu$ has no atom of mass $1$, split the support into two disjoint positive-measure pieces.) Then
$$(\mu\otimes\mu)(A\times B)=\mu(A)\,\mu(B)>0,$$
but $A\times B$ is disjoint from the diagonal $\{(x,y):x=y\}$, contradicting $(\dagger)$.

Hence $\mu$ must be a Dirac measure. ∎

---

### Conclusion

Both directions hold, so
$$\mu\text{ is not a Dirac measure}\iff|\hat{\mu}(\xi)|\not\equiv 1\text{ for }\xi\in\mathbb{R}.$$

The claim is **true**. (We note that the compact-support hypothesis is not actually needed; the argument works for any Borel probability measure on $\mathbb{R}$, since only the countability of $\mathbb{Q}$ and the bound $\cos\le 1$ are used.)

